nano: build colour and nanorc without libmagic, and give it a usable stack

Two changes to package/nano/nano.mk, both aimed at making the non-tiny build
actually usable on a NOMMU target.

package/nano/nano.mk enables --enable-color and --enable-nanorc only when
BR2_PACKAGE_FILE is selected, alongside --enable-libmagic. The three are
independent options in nano: libmagic is a fallback for identifying a file whose
extension and first line say nothing, while colour and nanorc are what make the
non-tiny build worth building. Bundling them costs the whole `file` package and
its magic database to get syntax highlighting.

The second is a larger stack. NOMMU has no stack guard page and no growth -- the
kernel allocates exactly what PT_GNU_STACK asks for -- and nano overflows the
default 32 kB during start-up once nanorc parsing and syntax regexes are on.

Signed-off-by: Mikhail Matveev <xtreme@rh1.tech>
--- a/package/nano/nano.mk
+++ b/package/nano/nano.mk
@@ -12,6 +12,8 @@
 NANO_LICENSE_FILES = COPYING
 NANO_DEPENDENCIES = ncurses
 
+NANO_CONF_ENV += LDFLAGS="$(TARGET_LDFLAGS) -Wl,-z,stack-size=131072"
+
 ifeq ($(BR2_PACKAGE_NCURSES_WCHAR),y)
 NANO_CONF_ENV += ac_cv_prog_NCURSESW_CONFIG=$(STAGING_DIR)/usr/bin/$(NCURSES_CONFIG_SCRIPTS)
 else
@@ -30,11 +32,38 @@
 endef
 else
 NANO_CONF_OPTS += --disable-tiny
+# Colour and nanorc do not need libmagic.
+#
+# They were bundled with it because syntax definitions can use `magic`
+# directives to identify a file with no recognisable extension. That is a
+# fallback, not a requirement: without libmagic the extension and first-line
+# rules still work, which is how nano decides for nearly every file. Tying them
+# together costs the whole `file` package and its several MB of magic data for
+# a feature most systems never reach -- and on a board with 8 MB of RAM that is
+# the difference between having syntax highlighting and not.
+NANO_CONF_OPTS += --enable-color --enable-nanorc
+
+# A bigger stack than the 32 kB the linker gives by default.
+#
+# NOMMU has no stack guard page and no growth: the kernel allocates exactly what
+# PT_GNU_STACK asks for, and going past it just writes over whatever is next.
+# nano overflows 32 kB during start-up once nanorc parsing and syntax regex
+# compilation are enabled -- observed as a jump to a garbage address with the
+# stack pointer 6 kB below its own base:
+#
+#   nano[35]: MPU fault, data access at 0x111e67d0 (MMFSR 0x92)
+#   PC is at 0x8400b0b0     <- not an address on this machine
+#   sp : 111e67e0           <- below the stack it was given
+#
+# and only when the stack happens to land next to memory the MPU protects. Off
+# an MMU-less system with no MPU it corrupts a neighbour instead and nobody
+# notices. 128 kB, not more: on NOMMU this is one contiguous allocation, and
+# large ones are exactly what fails first as memory fragments.
 ifeq ($(BR2_PACKAGE_FILE),y)
 NANO_DEPENDENCIES += file
-NANO_CONF_OPTS += --enable-libmagic --enable-color --enable-nanorc
+NANO_CONF_OPTS += --enable-libmagic
 else
-NANO_CONF_OPTS += --disable-libmagic --disable-color --disable-nanorc
+NANO_CONF_OPTS += --disable-libmagic
 endif # BR2_PACKAGE_FILE
 endif # BR2_PACKAGE_NANO_TINY
 
